1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,440
DeepSeek V3 holds 671 billion

2
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:08,920
parameters.

3
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:11,120
Push one token through it and only 37

4
00:00:08,920 --> 00:00:14,480
billion of them are multiplied against

5
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:17,720
that token, about 1/18 of the model.

6
00:00:14,480 --> 00:00:20,280
The other 634 billion sit in memory

7
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:23,240
untouched by this token waiting for the

8
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:26,280
other tokens that will need them.

9
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:28,960
That gap between what the model holds

10
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:31,880
and what any single token actually uses

11
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:33,920
is the core of Mixture of Experts or

12
00:00:31,880 --> 00:00:35,520
MoE.

13
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:37,760
By the end of this video, we will be

14
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:40,080
able to derive that split, which parts

15
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:42,160
of the model a token gets to skip, and

16
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:44,040
what the skipping costs everywhere else

17
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:46,280
in the system.

18
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:48,560
A dense model multiplies every token

19
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:51,160
against every weight, so there is no gap

20
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:53,600
at all. Making the model know more means

21
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:54,840
making every single token do more work,

22
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:56,280
whether [music] it needs that knowledge

23
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:58,800
or not.

24
00:00:56,280 --> 00:01:01,520
MoE is the architecture that separates

25
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:04,720
the two, how much a model can know and

26
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:07,640
how much of it runs for each token.

27
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:10,200
Why would anyone build a model with 671

28
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:13,080
billion parameters in the first place?

29
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:17,160
The answer is scaling laws.

30
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:19,560
In 2020, Jared Kaplan and his colleagues

31
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:21,600
measured how a language model's loss,

32
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:24,720
the error it makes predicting the next

33
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:27,115
token, falls as you grow the model, the

34
00:01:24,720 --> 00:01:27,560
data, and the training compute.

35
00:01:27,115 --> 00:01:30,520
>> [music]

36
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:33,120
>> It falls as a power law in all three.

37
00:01:30,520 --> 00:01:35,600
On logarithmic axis, the messy curve

38
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:37,880
becomes a straight line sloping down,

39
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:40,280
which means you can forecast how much

40
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:43,120
better a bigger model will get before

41
00:01:40,280 --> 00:01:45,560
you spend anything building it.

42
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:48,000
Two years later, the Chinchilla paper

43
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:50,480
from DeepMind corrected the recipe

44
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,800
without changing its direction. For a

45
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:55,200
fixed compute budget, the model and its

46
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:57,440
training data should grow together, and

47
00:01:55,200 --> 00:02:00,560
many models of that era turned out to be

48
00:01:57,440 --> 00:02:03,440
oversized for the data they were fed.

49
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:06,200
Feed a bigger model enough data and its

50
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:08,160
loss falls predictably.

51
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:11,320
That predictability is the pressure that

52
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:13,000
keeps pushing parameter counts up.

53
00:02:11,320 --> 00:02:15,880
That growth runs straight into the

54
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,600
memory wall from episode one.

55
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:21,680
At decode, producing each new token

56
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:24,040
means reading every weight out of HPM,

57
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:26,400
the chip's high-bandwidth memory, and

58
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:28,400
streaming it across the memory bus into

59
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:30,600
the arithmetic units.

60
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:32,880
The multiplications themselves are

61
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:35,560
almost free. When we measured this in

62
00:02:32,880 --> 00:02:38,440
episode one, arithmetic came out to less

63
00:02:35,560 --> 00:02:40,560
than 1% of each token's time. The pace

64
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:42,720
is set by the reading.

65
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:46,880
And in a dense model, the amount you

66
00:02:42,720 --> 00:02:51,160
have to read is the whole model. A 671

67
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:53,480
billion parameter model streams all 671

68
00:02:51,160 --> 00:02:56,600
billion weights for every token it

69
00:02:53,480 --> 00:02:58,920
produces, which means the time per token

70
00:02:56,600 --> 00:03:00,720
grows in lockstep with the parameter

71
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:02,920
count.

72
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:05,320
So, the same growth that scaling laws

73
00:03:02,920 --> 00:03:08,440
reward is exactly what the memory wall

74
00:03:05,320 --> 00:03:11,400
punishes. Every parameter you add makes

75
00:03:08,440 --> 00:03:15,480
every token slower to produce.

76
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:18,000
MoE is the architectural response.

77
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:20,920
To run only part of the model for each

78
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:23,480
token, we first need to know where the

79
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:25,519
knowledge actually lives.

80
00:03:23,480 --> 00:03:26,840
A transformer layer does two different

81
00:03:25,519 --> 00:03:28,920
jobs.

82
00:03:26,840 --> 00:03:31,519
Attention is the part that mixes

83
00:03:28,920 --> 00:03:34,200
information across the sequence. It

84
00:03:31,519 --> 00:03:36,200
looks at every position and decides what

85
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:39,880
is relevant to what.

86
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:42,400
The feed-forward network, the FFN,

87
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:44,959
is the part that processes each token on

88
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:47,040
its own, the same transformation applied

89
00:03:44,959 --> 00:03:48,640
at every position.

90
00:03:47,040 --> 00:03:50,360
You would expect attention, the

91
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:51,840
clever-looking half, to be where the

92
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:55,360
knowledge is stored.

93
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:58,520
It turns out to be the FFN.

94
00:03:55,360 --> 00:04:00,440
In 2021, Mo Gewa and colleagues showed

95
00:03:58,520 --> 00:04:03,040
what a feedforward layer is actually

96
00:04:00,440 --> 00:04:04,480
doing. It behaves like an enormous soft

97
00:04:03,040 --> 00:04:06,400
lookup table.

98
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:08,840
Each key recognizes a pattern in the

99
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:11,240
input, something like the capital of

100
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:13,080
France is, and the matching value pushes

101
00:04:11,240 --> 00:04:15,920
the model's output toward particular

102
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:17,600
tokens, like Paris.

103
00:04:15,920 --> 00:04:20,519
When the model completes that sentence

104
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:22,002
correctly, it is the FFN doing the

105
00:04:20,519 --> 00:04:22,200
remembering.

106
00:04:22,002 --> 00:04:24,880
>> [music]

107
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:27,280
>> And the scale matters. About 2/3 of a

108
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:29,320
transformer's parameters sit in its

109
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:32,080
feedforward layers.

110
00:04:29,320 --> 00:04:34,280
The FFN is simultaneously where the

111
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:36,640
recall happens and where most of the

112
00:04:34,280 --> 00:04:38,378
model lives, which makes it the obvious

113
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:38,720
thing to sparsify.

114
00:04:38,378 --> 00:04:42,400
>> [music]

115
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:45,000
>> Attention is left exactly as it was.

116
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:47,400
The change MoE makes to the transformer

117
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,440
is surprisingly local.

118
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:52,600
Where a dense layer runs one feedforward

119
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:55,680
network, an MoE layer holds N of them

120
00:04:52,600 --> 00:04:58,919
side by side, called experts, and each

121
00:04:55,680 --> 00:05:00,200
expert is a complete full-size FFN of

122
00:04:58,919 --> 00:05:02,520
its own.

123
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:05,000
In front of them sits a small router,

124
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:09,120
also called the gate, whose whole job is

125
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:11,160
to decide which experts see which token.

126
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:13,240
For every token that arrives, the router

127
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:16,040
produces a score for each of the N

128
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:18,400
experts, keeps the K highest scorers,

129
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:21,400
and sends the token through only those.

130
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:23,440
The classic choice is K equals 2.

131
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:25,680
The outputs of those two experts are

132
00:05:23,440 --> 00:05:27,720
then blended into a single result, a

133
00:05:25,680 --> 00:05:30,800
weighted sum, and the weights are the

134
00:05:27,720 --> 00:05:32,280
router's own scores, renormalized.

135
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:34,560
So, the same numbers that chose the

136
00:05:32,280 --> 00:05:36,720
experts also decide how much each one

137
00:05:34,560 --> 00:05:38,680
contributes.

138
00:05:36,720 --> 00:05:40,240
Now, look at what that structure does to

139
00:05:38,680 --> 00:05:42,720
the accounting.

140
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:44,680
Total parameters grow with N because

141
00:05:42,720 --> 00:05:47,120
every expert has to stay resident in

142
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:49,800
memory whether or not this token used

143
00:05:47,120 --> 00:05:53,480
it. But compute per token grows only

144
00:05:49,800 --> 00:05:55,600
with K because only K experts ever run.

145
00:05:53,480 --> 00:05:57,280
N and [music] K become two independent

146
00:05:55,600 --> 00:06:00,200
dials.

147
00:05:57,280 --> 00:06:02,920
Mistral, Mistral's open model, makes the

148
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:06,440
split concrete. Eight experts with

149
00:06:02,920 --> 00:06:09,960
top-two routing gives 46.7 billion total

150
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:13,040
parameters, of which only 12.9 billion

151
00:06:09,960 --> 00:06:15,320
are used for any given token.

152
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:17,560
There is one claim about MoE that people

153
00:06:15,320 --> 00:06:19,120
get wrong more than any other. So, it is

154
00:06:17,560 --> 00:06:22,160
worth being precise about what the

155
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:24,080
sparsity did and did not touch.

156
00:06:22,160 --> 00:06:26,720
Everything we just sparsified lives

157
00:06:24,080 --> 00:06:29,040
inside the feed-forward block.

158
00:06:26,720 --> 00:06:31,120
Attention is still fully dense. Every

159
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:33,280
token still attends over every token

160
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:35,360
before it. That work still grows with

161
00:06:33,280 --> 00:06:37,720
the length of the context, and none of

162
00:06:35,360 --> 00:06:39,919
it consults the router.

163
00:06:37,720 --> 00:06:42,520
Which means the KV cache, the stored

164
00:06:39,919 --> 00:06:44,240
keys and values for every prior token,

165
00:06:42,520 --> 00:06:46,360
is exactly what it was in the dense

166
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:48,880
model, growing with the context at

167
00:06:46,360 --> 00:06:51,520
exactly the same rate.

168
00:06:48,880 --> 00:06:54,440
So, MoE makes the FFN compute cheaper

169
00:06:51,520 --> 00:06:57,000
per token, and that is all it does.

170
00:06:54,440 --> 00:06:59,120
If your problem is long context, if the

171
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:02,640
KV cache is what is overflowing your

172
00:06:59,120 --> 00:07:04,720
memory, MoE does not help you at all.

173
00:07:02,640 --> 00:07:06,640
That memory wall stands as tall as it

174
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:09,040
did before.

175
00:07:06,640 --> 00:07:12,800
You can see the proof in DeepSeek v3

176
00:07:09,040 --> 00:07:15,120
itself. Alongside its MoE layers, it

177
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:17,720
runs a separate attention technique,

178
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:21,600
multi-head attention, which attacks

179
00:07:17,720 --> 00:07:23,800
exactly the cost MoE leaves untouched,

180
00:07:21,600 --> 00:07:26,160
the KV cache.

181
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:29,160
So, when you read 671

182
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:31,160
billion total against 37 billion active,

183
00:07:29,160 --> 00:07:33,440
remember that the ratio comes from the

184
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:36,440
FFN alone.

185
00:07:33,440 --> 00:07:38,280
Attention runs in full on every single

186
00:07:36,440 --> 00:07:40,320
token.

187
00:07:38,280 --> 00:07:42,880
Now, we can open the router itself

188
00:07:40,320 --> 00:07:45,160
because so far it has been a black box

189
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:47,000
that somehow knows which experts match

190
00:07:45,160 --> 00:07:49,320
which token.

191
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:51,080
The router never sees the word. It reads

192
00:07:49,320 --> 00:07:53,040
the token's hidden vector, the

193
00:07:51,080 --> 00:07:55,480
representation the layer has built up

194
00:07:53,040 --> 00:07:57,560
for that token so far, and runs three

195
00:07:55,480 --> 00:08:00,560
small steps on it.

196
00:07:57,560 --> 00:08:03,040
First, a small learned linear layer, one

197
00:08:00,560 --> 00:08:06,520
matrix, turns that vector into one raw

198
00:08:03,040 --> 00:08:10,080
score per expert, n numbers in a row.

199
00:08:06,520 --> 00:08:12,680
Then, a softmax turns those raw scores

200
00:08:10,080 --> 00:08:15,560
into weights between zero and one that

201
00:08:12,680 --> 00:08:17,800
sum to exactly one. So, they behave like

202
00:08:15,560 --> 00:08:20,200
shares of a fixed budget.

203
00:08:17,800 --> 00:08:22,280
Finally, top k keeps the k largest

204
00:08:20,200 --> 00:08:23,960
shares, zeros out the rest, and

205
00:08:22,280 --> 00:08:26,720
re-normalizes the survivors so the

206
00:08:23,960 --> 00:08:29,240
budget adds back up to one.

207
00:08:26,720 --> 00:08:31,880
Those k surviving weights are the same

208
00:08:29,240 --> 00:08:34,159
coefficients we met in the weighted sum.

209
00:08:31,880 --> 00:08:36,680
The numbers that selected the experts

210
00:08:34,159 --> 00:08:39,000
now blend their outputs.

211
00:08:36,680 --> 00:08:42,200
And notice how little work this took.

212
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:44,520
One small matrix multiply plus a softmax

213
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:47,200
sitting in front of the enormous expert

214
00:08:44,520 --> 00:08:49,760
feed forward networks it controls.

215
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:52,040
The router's cost rounds to zero, which

216
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:55,920
is exactly what lets the model afford to

217
00:08:52,040 --> 00:08:58,760
run it on every token at every layer.

218
00:08:55,920 --> 00:09:00,960
One detail worth knowing, some models,

219
00:08:58,760 --> 00:09:03,160
Deep Seek V3 among them, compute their

220
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:06,560
affinity scores with a sigmoid plus a

221
00:09:03,160 --> 00:09:08,800
normalization instead of a softmax.

222
00:09:06,560 --> 00:09:11,680
The skeleton stays the same, score,

223
00:09:08,800 --> 00:09:13,440
select, re-normalize.

224
00:09:11,680 --> 00:09:14,136
So, how many experts should a layer

225
00:09:13,440 --> 00:09:14,200
have,

226
00:09:14,136 --> 00:09:17,120
>> [music]

227
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:19,839
>> and how big should each one be?

228
00:09:17,120 --> 00:09:22,680
The early answer was a few large ones.

229
00:09:19,839 --> 00:09:25,480
Mixture's eight is that recipe.

230
00:09:22,680 --> 00:09:28,480
The modern answer is many small ones,

231
00:09:25,480 --> 00:09:30,760
and the reason is combinatorial.

232
00:09:28,480 --> 00:09:33,000
Start with 16 experts and top two

233
00:09:30,760 --> 00:09:36,160
routing, and count the ways a token can

234
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:38,760
be served. It gets some pair of experts,

235
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:41,000
and there are only 120 possible pairs.

236
00:09:38,760 --> 00:09:43,360
So, every token the model will ever see

237
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:44,960
has to be handled by one of those 120

238
00:09:43,360 --> 00:09:48,040
teams.

239
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:50,880
Now, cut each expert into four slivers,

240
00:09:48,040 --> 00:09:53,436
each a quarter of the original width.

241
00:09:50,880 --> 00:09:55,480
That gives 64 smaller experts, and

242
00:09:53,436 --> 00:09:57,800
[music] to keep the compute identical,

243
00:09:55,480 --> 00:10:00,080
you route each token to eight of them

244
00:09:57,800 --> 00:10:02,240
because eight quarter-width experts cost

245
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:04,080
exactly what two full-width experts

246
00:10:02,240 --> 00:10:06,640
cost.

247
00:10:04,080 --> 00:10:09,600
The compute did not move, but the number

248
00:10:06,640 --> 00:10:12,920
of possible teams jumped from 120 to

249
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:16,560
over 4 billion. Choosing eight out of 64

250
00:10:12,920 --> 00:10:19,200
gives about 4.4 billion combinations,

251
00:10:16,560 --> 00:10:21,200
roughly 37 million times as many ways to

252
00:10:19,200 --> 00:10:23,080
assemble knowledge for a token at the

253
00:10:21,200 --> 00:10:26,000
same price.

254
00:10:23,080 --> 00:10:28,920
This move, many small experts instead of

255
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:31,880
a few big ones, is the fine-grained idea

256
00:10:28,920 --> 00:10:36,200
from the DeepSeek MoE paper.

257
00:10:31,880 --> 00:10:38,240
DeepSeek V3 runs it at 256

258
00:10:36,200 --> 00:10:40,760
routed experts for the router to pick

259
00:10:38,240 --> 00:10:44,280
from, with eight firing for any given

260
00:10:40,760 --> 00:10:46,880
token, and DeepSeek V4 Pro, the model we

261
00:10:44,280 --> 00:10:49,560
met in episode 5, pushes the pool to

262
00:10:46,880 --> 00:10:52,120
384.

263
00:10:49,560 --> 00:10:54,160
Compute per token barely moves, while

264
00:10:52,120 --> 00:10:56,317
what a token can compose becomes far

265
00:10:54,160 --> 00:10:56,360
more specific.

266
00:10:56,317 --> 00:10:59,200
>> [music]

267
00:10:56,360 --> 00:11:01,680
>> The DeepSeek MoE paper added a second

268
00:10:59,200 --> 00:11:04,120
refinement, and it starts from a waste

269
00:11:01,680 --> 00:11:05,640
problem hiding inside the design we just

270
00:11:04,120 --> 00:11:07,720
built.

271
00:11:05,640 --> 00:11:09,120
Some knowledge is useful for almost

272
00:11:07,720 --> 00:11:12,160
every token.

273
00:11:09,120 --> 00:11:15,440
Basic syntax, frequent patterns, the

274
00:11:12,160 --> 00:11:17,360
things almost every sentence needs.

275
00:11:15,440 --> 00:11:20,120
But, the router scatters tokens across

276
00:11:17,360 --> 00:11:22,520
the pool, so every routed expert keeps

277
00:11:20,120 --> 00:11:25,200
meeting that common material, and every

278
00:11:22,520 --> 00:11:27,400
one of them has to learn its own copy.

279
00:11:25,200 --> 00:11:29,960
The same basics end up duplicated across

280
00:11:27,400 --> 00:11:33,360
the whole pool, eating capacity that was

281
00:11:29,960 --> 00:11:35,160
supposed to hold specialized knowledge.

282
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:38,000
The fix is to give the common work a

283
00:11:35,160 --> 00:11:41,040
permanent home. A shared expert runs for

284
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:42,760
every token alongside the routed top K,

285
00:11:41,040 --> 00:11:43,960
and it never goes through the router at

286
00:11:42,760 --> 00:11:46,280
all.

287
00:11:43,960 --> 00:11:48,063
Because it sees everything, the common

288
00:11:46,280 --> 00:11:50,320
patterns settle there, and the routed

289
00:11:48,063 --> 00:11:53,960
[music] experts, freed from relearning

290
00:11:50,320 --> 00:11:56,840
the basics, can actually specialize.

291
00:11:53,960 --> 00:11:59,520
DeepSeek V3 pairs one shared expert with

292
00:11:56,840 --> 00:12:01,880
its 256 routed ones.

293
00:11:59,520 --> 00:12:04,280
LLaMA 4 Maverick does the same, and so

294
00:12:01,880 --> 00:12:05,320
does Chimera 2, the trillion parameter

295
00:12:04,280 --> 00:12:06,520
model.

296
00:12:05,320 --> 00:12:09,800
Qwen 3,

297
00:12:06,520 --> 00:12:12,160
at 235 billion parameters, deliberately

298
00:12:09,800 --> 00:12:14,480
leaves the shared expert out.

299
00:12:12,160 --> 00:12:16,520
So, it's a popular refinement, but not a

300
00:12:14,480 --> 00:12:19,200
requirement.

301
00:12:16,520 --> 00:12:20,760
Everything so far has assumed the token

302
00:12:19,200 --> 00:12:23,560
does the choosing.

303
00:12:20,760 --> 00:12:26,560
Each token picks its top K experts, and

304
00:12:23,560 --> 00:12:28,760
the experts take whatever arrives.

305
00:12:26,560 --> 00:12:31,320
That design is called token choice

306
00:12:28,760 --> 00:12:33,240
routing, and nearly every deployed model

307
00:12:31,320 --> 00:12:35,520
works this way.

308
00:12:33,240 --> 00:12:37,240
But it never promises balance. Nothing

309
00:12:35,520 --> 00:12:39,720
anywhere forces the traffic to spread

310
00:12:37,240 --> 00:12:42,360
evenly, so one expert can end up flooded

311
00:12:39,720 --> 00:12:44,000
while another sits idle.

312
00:12:42,360 --> 00:12:46,080
In 2022,

313
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:48,000
Zhu and colleagues proposed turning the

314
00:12:46,080 --> 00:12:50,440
whole thing around.

315
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:52,960
In expert choice routing, each expert

316
00:12:50,440 --> 00:12:55,600
picks its own top C tokens up to a fixed

317
00:12:52,960 --> 00:12:56,680
capacity, a per batch cap on how many it

318
00:12:55,600 --> 00:12:59,080
will take.

319
00:12:56,680 --> 00:13:01,520
Fill every expert to its cap, and the

320
00:12:59,080 --> 00:13:04,120
load is balanced by construction.

321
00:13:01,520 --> 00:13:07,080
Overload is impossible because no expert

322
00:13:04,120 --> 00:13:09,760
can accept more than its share.

323
00:13:07,080 --> 00:13:12,160
So, why doesn't everyone route this way?

324
00:13:09,760 --> 00:13:13,720
There are two problems, one gentle and

325
00:13:12,160 --> 00:13:15,839
one fatal.

326
00:13:13,720 --> 00:13:18,040
The gentler one is that a token nobody

327
00:13:15,839 --> 00:13:19,360
picks simply gets skipped for that

328
00:13:18,040 --> 00:13:21,440
layer.

329
00:13:19,360 --> 00:13:23,640
The fatal one is causality. For an

330
00:13:21,440 --> 00:13:25,800
expert to rank its tokens, it has to

331
00:13:23,640 --> 00:13:27,880
compare them across the whole sequence,

332
00:13:25,800 --> 00:13:30,320
including positions the decoder has not

333
00:13:27,880 --> 00:13:32,480
generated yet. Tokens that at decode

334
00:13:30,320 --> 00:13:34,400
time do not exist.

335
00:13:32,480 --> 00:13:36,760
So, production decoding sticks with

336
00:13:34,400 --> 00:13:38,960
token choice and lives with the uneven

337
00:13:36,760 --> 00:13:41,840
traffic because a router that needs to

338
00:13:38,960 --> 00:13:45,400
see the future is no use in a decoder

339
00:13:41,840 --> 00:13:47,800
that produces one token at a time.

340
00:13:45,400 --> 00:13:49,920
Token choice has a failure mode and it

341
00:13:47,800 --> 00:13:52,200
is almost inevitable.

342
00:13:49,920 --> 00:13:54,240
A rich get richer loop the field calls

343
00:13:52,200 --> 00:13:57,280
routing collapse.

344
00:13:54,240 --> 00:13:59,320
Early in training, purely by luck, a few

345
00:13:57,280 --> 00:14:00,840
experts get slightly better at some

346
00:13:59,320 --> 00:14:03,480
common pattern.

347
00:14:00,840 --> 00:14:05,280
The router notices and sends them a

348
00:14:03,480 --> 00:14:06,960
little more traffic.

349
00:14:05,280 --> 00:14:10,080
More traffic means more gradient

350
00:14:06,960 --> 00:14:11,960
updates, so they improve faster. And the

351
00:14:10,080 --> 00:14:13,640
better they get, the more the router

352
00:14:11,960 --> 00:14:15,760
prefers them.

353
00:14:13,640 --> 00:14:17,840
Left alone, that loop ends with a few

354
00:14:15,760 --> 00:14:20,040
overloaded celebrity experts handling

355
00:14:17,840 --> 00:14:22,520
most of the traffic while a long tail of

356
00:14:20,040 --> 00:14:24,320
dead experts almost never fires.

357
00:14:22,520 --> 00:14:25,960
Capacity you are paying to store but

358
00:14:24,320 --> 00:14:28,160
never using.

359
00:14:25,960 --> 00:14:30,800
And in a cluster, it gets worse because

360
00:14:28,160 --> 00:14:33,080
the GPU holding a hot expert becomes a

361
00:14:30,800 --> 00:14:35,000
straggler that every other GPU waits

362
00:14:33,080 --> 00:14:37,520
for.

363
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:39,839
The classic fix introduced by Shazeer

364
00:14:37,520 --> 00:14:41,440
and refined in the Switch Transformer is

365
00:14:39,839 --> 00:14:44,040
to add a second term to the training

366
00:14:41,440 --> 00:14:45,760
loss. An auxiliary loss that is smallest

367
00:14:44,040 --> 00:14:47,280
when traffic spreads evenly across the

368
00:14:45,760 --> 00:14:49,960
experts.

369
00:14:47,280 --> 00:14:52,600
Switch, which routes each token to just

370
00:14:49,960 --> 00:14:54,560
one expert, set that terms weight to

371
00:14:52,600 --> 00:14:56,000
0.01.

372
00:14:54,560 --> 00:14:58,560
Large enough to keep the experts

373
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:00,800
balanced, small enough not to overwhelm

374
00:14:58,560 --> 00:15:02,560
the real objective.

375
00:15:00,800 --> 00:15:04,565
But the balancing gradient has nothing

376
00:15:02,560 --> 00:15:04,880
to do with next token prediction.

377
00:15:04,565 --> 00:15:06,800
>> [music]

378
00:15:04,880 --> 00:15:09,480
>> When the genuinely best expert for a

379
00:15:06,800 --> 00:15:11,480
token happens to be a popular one, it

380
00:15:09,480 --> 00:15:12,800
pushes the router away from that expert

381
00:15:11,480 --> 00:15:14,640
anyway.

382
00:15:12,800 --> 00:15:18,480
Balance comes at the cost of a little

383
00:15:14,640 --> 00:15:20,640
model quality on every update.

384
00:15:18,480 --> 00:15:23,720
DeepSeek's answer from Wang and

385
00:15:20,640 --> 00:15:26,040
colleagues in 2024 is to stop using a

386
00:15:23,720 --> 00:15:28,400
gradient for balance altogether and use

387
00:15:26,040 --> 00:15:30,880
a feedback controller instead.

388
00:15:28,400 --> 00:15:33,480
Every expert gets a small bias added to

389
00:15:30,880 --> 00:15:35,920
its router score, and that bias is used

390
00:15:33,480 --> 00:15:38,880
only for the top K selection, never in

391
00:15:35,920 --> 00:15:41,040
the weighted sum. After each batch, the

392
00:15:38,880 --> 00:15:43,440
controller counts what every expert

393
00:15:41,040 --> 00:15:46,080
received, nudges the bias up for the

394
00:15:43,440 --> 00:15:49,440
starved ones and down for the overloaded

395
00:15:46,080 --> 00:15:51,880
ones by a fixed step. And over time, the

396
00:15:49,440 --> 00:15:54,800
biases settle where traffic spreads

397
00:15:51,880 --> 00:15:57,520
evenly. The language gradient is never

398
00:15:54,800 --> 00:15:59,920
touched. And note the careful detail.

399
00:15:57,520 --> 00:16:02,480
The controller only ever reads past

400
00:15:59,920 --> 00:16:04,680
batches because reading the current one

401
00:16:02,480 --> 00:16:08,000
would leak future tokens, the same

402
00:16:04,680 --> 00:16:09,920
causality constraint we just met.

403
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:11,280
This improves balance without closing

404
00:16:09,920 --> 00:16:13,440
the problem.

405
00:16:11,280 --> 00:16:15,560
A single sequence can still hammer a few

406
00:16:13,440 --> 00:16:17,840
experts even when the batch average

407
00:16:15,560 --> 00:16:19,960
looks fine, and serving traffic never

408
00:16:17,840 --> 00:16:21,320
quite matches what training balanced

409
00:16:19,960 --> 00:16:23,240
for.

410
00:16:21,320 --> 00:16:27,200
Load balancing stays an operational

411
00:16:23,240 --> 00:16:29,160
concern, watched and patched at runtime.

412
00:16:27,200 --> 00:16:31,680
All of this made the model cheaper per

413
00:16:29,160 --> 00:16:34,360
token. And this is where we pay for it

414
00:16:31,680 --> 00:16:37,120
somewhere else because sparsity pushes

415
00:16:34,360 --> 00:16:38,880
its difficulty into serving.

416
00:16:37,120 --> 00:16:43,080
Start with the memory.

417
00:16:38,880 --> 00:16:46,880
671 billion parameters stored in FP8, 1

418
00:16:43,080 --> 00:16:49,160
byte each, come to about 671 GB of

419
00:16:46,880 --> 00:16:53,440
weights, and that is too big even for

420
00:16:49,160 --> 00:16:55,520
1/8 GPU machine of 80 GB cards.

421
00:16:53,440 --> 00:16:58,160
The experts have to spread across a

422
00:16:55,520 --> 00:16:59,640
cluster, and that arrangement is expert

423
00:16:58,160 --> 00:17:01,839
parallelism.

424
00:16:59,640 --> 00:17:04,000
Where the tensor parallelism of episode

425
00:17:01,839 --> 00:17:06,880
5 slices every weight matrix into

426
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:09,600
pieces, expert parallelism keeps each

427
00:17:06,880 --> 00:17:11,480
expert whole and gives it a home on some

428
00:17:09,600 --> 00:17:14,240
GPU.

429
00:17:11,480 --> 00:17:15,640
DeepSeek V3's decode deployment shows

430
00:17:14,240 --> 00:17:18,160
the scale.

431
00:17:15,640 --> 00:17:22,120
320 GPUs

432
00:17:18,160 --> 00:17:26,000
the 256 routed experts spread at one per

433
00:17:22,120 --> 00:17:28,480
GPU, and the remaining 64 GPUs holding

434
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:31,160
the shared expert and redundant copies

435
00:17:28,480 --> 00:17:34,040
of the hottest experts, so no single

436
00:17:31,160 --> 00:17:37,040
chip becomes the choke point.

437
00:17:34,040 --> 00:17:39,560
Now, follow one token through a layer.

438
00:17:37,040 --> 00:17:41,920
It sits on some GPU, its router picks

439
00:17:39,560 --> 00:17:44,240
eight experts, and those experts live

440
00:17:41,920 --> 00:17:47,160
mostly on other machines.

441
00:17:44,240 --> 00:17:50,400
So, every MoE layer runs two all-to-all

442
00:17:47,160 --> 00:17:52,880
exchanges, a dispatch which ships each

443
00:17:50,400 --> 00:17:53,557
token out to the GPUs holding its chosen

444
00:17:52,880 --> 00:17:53,880
experts,

445
00:17:53,557 --> 00:17:56,000
>> [music]

446
00:17:53,880 --> 00:17:57,480
>> and a combine which carries the results

447
00:17:56,000 --> 00:18:00,080
back home.

448
00:17:57,480 --> 00:18:02,000
Both sit on the decode critical path,

449
00:18:00,080 --> 00:18:05,800
the [music] time per output token we met

450
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:07,600
in episode one, and before optimization,

451
00:18:05,800 --> 00:18:09,800
DeepSeek's training runs showed

452
00:18:07,600 --> 00:18:13,000
computation and communication at roughly

453
00:18:09,800 --> 00:18:15,827
one-to-one, for every moment computing,

454
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:16,160
a moment waiting on the network.

455
00:18:15,827 --> 00:18:18,440
>> [music]

456
00:18:16,160 --> 00:18:21,360
>> Meanwhile, every expert stays resident

457
00:18:18,440 --> 00:18:24,000
in memory, busy or not. That is why the

458
00:18:21,360 --> 00:18:25,000
economics hinge on concurrency.

459
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:28,280
It takes [music] thousands of

460
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:30,680
simultaneous requests before all 256

461
00:18:28,280 --> 00:18:32,720
experts have real work, and even in

462
00:18:30,680 --> 00:18:35,880
DeepSeek's deployment, each expert's

463
00:18:32,720 --> 00:18:38,760
batch usually stays within 256 tokens,

464
00:18:35,880 --> 00:18:41,040
still limited by memory access.

465
00:18:38,760 --> 00:18:45,040
At batch one, you get the worst of both

466
00:18:41,040 --> 00:18:48,040
sides, the full 671 GB resident, eight

467
00:18:45,040 --> 00:18:51,400
experts working, and a KV cache exactly

468
00:18:48,040 --> 00:18:53,720
as large as a dense model would carry.

469
00:18:51,400 --> 00:18:56,760
Left unchecked, those two all-to-alls

470
00:18:53,720 --> 00:18:59,240
per layer would sink decode latency, so

471
00:18:56,760 --> 00:19:00,880
MoE serving leans on three tricks to

472
00:18:59,240 --> 00:19:03,280
tame them.

473
00:19:00,880 --> 00:19:05,240
The first is node-limited routing, and

474
00:19:03,280 --> 00:19:08,400
it exploits the shape of the hardware we

475
00:19:05,240 --> 00:19:10,800
mapped in episode five. GPUs inside one

476
00:19:08,400 --> 00:19:12,720
machine talk over fast links, while

477
00:19:10,800 --> 00:19:14,440
machines talk to each other over much

478
00:19:12,720 --> 00:19:16,720
slower ones.

479
00:19:14,440 --> 00:19:20,480
So, constrain the router.

480
00:19:16,720 --> 00:19:23,280
DeepSeek V3 arranges its 256 experts

481
00:19:20,480 --> 00:19:25,960
into eight groups, one per node, and

482
00:19:23,280 --> 00:19:28,280
caps every token at four nodes, which

483
00:19:25,960 --> 00:19:30,520
keeps most of the dispatch and combine

484
00:19:28,280 --> 00:19:32,440
traffic inside machines instead of

485
00:19:30,520 --> 00:19:35,000
between them.

486
00:19:32,440 --> 00:19:37,240
The second trick is overlap. Run two

487
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:39,600
microbatches through the layer at once,

488
00:19:37,240 --> 00:19:41,840
staggered, so that while one of them is

489
00:19:39,600 --> 00:19:44,040
computing, the other is in flight on the

490
00:19:41,840 --> 00:19:46,080
network. The communication still

491
00:19:44,040 --> 00:19:49,960
happens, it just happens behind the

492
00:19:46,080 --> 00:19:51,800
compute, where nobody is waiting on it.

493
00:19:49,960 --> 00:19:54,440
The third trick deals with imbalance

494
00:19:51,800 --> 00:19:57,000
directly. Serving traffic never spreads

495
00:19:54,440 --> 00:19:59,800
perfectly, so the hottest experts get

496
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:01,880
duplicate copies on spare GPUs, and a

497
00:19:59,800 --> 00:20:02,960
popular experts queue can be split in

498
00:20:01,880 --> 00:20:05,400
half.

499
00:20:02,960 --> 00:20:08,840
DeepSeek V3's prefill deployment carries

500
00:20:05,400 --> 00:20:11,840
32 of these redundant experts.

501
00:20:08,840 --> 00:20:14,240
Notice what none of this removes.

502
00:20:11,840 --> 00:20:17,360
The bytes still cross the network, every

503
00:20:14,240 --> 00:20:19,040
MoE layer, every token, exactly as many

504
00:20:17,360 --> 00:20:21,480
as before.

505
00:20:19,040 --> 00:20:23,800
The three tricks only take that movement

506
00:20:21,480 --> 00:20:27,360
off the critical path, so the time per

507
00:20:23,800 --> 00:20:28,920
output token stops depending on it.

508
00:20:27,360 --> 00:20:32,920
Recall the number this video opened

509
00:20:28,920 --> 00:20:35,400
with, 671 billion parameters held, 37

510
00:20:32,920 --> 00:20:38,560
billion run for any single token. We now

511
00:20:35,400 --> 00:20:40,160
have every piece needed to derive it.

512
00:20:38,560 --> 00:20:44,040
The knowledge lives in the feedforward

513
00:20:40,160 --> 00:20:47,789
network, so that is what MoE splits.

514
00:20:44,040 --> 00:20:49,840
In DeepSeek V3, 256 routed experts

515
00:20:47,789 --> 00:20:51,760
[music] plus one shared expert in every

516
00:20:49,840 --> 00:20:53,029
MoE layer.

517
00:20:51,760 --> 00:20:53,240
When a token arrives,

518
00:20:53,029 --> 00:20:54,920
>> [music]

519
00:20:53,240 --> 00:20:56,920
>> the router scores the whole pool and

520
00:20:54,920 --> 00:20:58,840
runs just the top eight. The shared

521
00:20:56,920 --> 00:21:01,360
expert runs alongside them, and

522
00:20:58,840 --> 00:21:03,280
attention runs in full, exactly as it

523
00:21:01,360 --> 00:21:05,400
would in a dense model.

524
00:21:03,280 --> 00:21:09,800
Count what actually fired, and it comes

525
00:21:05,400 --> 00:21:11,960
to 37 billion parameters out of 671.

526
00:21:09,800 --> 00:21:14,760
A cluster holds the full set, every

527
00:21:11,960 --> 00:21:17,640
expert resident on some GPU, and ships

528
00:21:14,760 --> 00:21:19,400
each token to the experts it picked.

529
00:21:17,640 --> 00:21:21,680
So, per token compute stays near the

530
00:21:19,400 --> 00:21:24,280
active 37 billion, while the stored

531
00:21:21,680 --> 00:21:26,040
knowledge tracks the full 671,

532
00:21:24,280 --> 00:21:27,760
and that same decoupling is what lets

533
00:21:26,040 --> 00:21:30,400
totals keep climbing.

534
00:21:27,760 --> 00:21:33,280
The early DeepSeek V4 Pro figures reach

535
00:21:30,400 --> 00:21:35,160
1.6 trillion total parameters, with the

536
00:21:33,280 --> 00:21:36,880
active count still in the tens of

537
00:21:35,160 --> 00:21:39,120
billions.

538
00:21:36,880 --> 00:21:41,800
Just do not read those headline totals

539
00:21:39,120 --> 00:21:44,440
as raw capability. The memory footprint

540
00:21:41,800 --> 00:21:46,560
still tracks every parameter, even as

541
00:21:44,440 --> 00:21:49,080
each token runs through only a handful

542
00:21:46,560 --> 00:21:51,520
of experts.

543
00:21:49,080 --> 00:21:53,920
Over six episodes, this series has been

544
00:21:51,520 --> 00:21:55,520
assembling an inference stack piece by

545
00:21:53,920 --> 00:21:57,720
piece.

546
00:21:55,520 --> 00:21:59,640
We started at the memory wall, went down

547
00:21:57,720 --> 00:22:01,600
into the hardware and the kernels,

548
00:21:59,640 --> 00:22:03,920
shrank the weights to fewer bits, and

549
00:22:01,600 --> 00:22:07,040
split the model across GPUs. This

550
00:22:03,920 --> 00:22:10,480
episode made its compute sparse.

551
00:22:07,040 --> 00:22:12,960
But, sparsity makes a quiet assumption.

552
00:22:10,480 --> 00:22:16,320
When one request arrives, eight experts

553
00:22:12,960 --> 00:22:18,360
do a little work, and the other 248 do

554
00:22:16,320 --> 00:22:20,280
nothing at all.

555
00:22:18,360 --> 00:22:22,600
For any of this to make sense, there

556
00:22:20,280 --> 00:22:24,880
have to be enough users on the system at

557
